WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
What the project asks, and what it doesn’t.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:18.264
The studio project is to fine-tune a small open model with LoRA for an extraction task, then benchmark it honestly against a frontier model and against the untuned base model, on quality, cost, and latency, and publish a model card for your adapter.

3
00:00:18.544 --> 00:00:22.192
The full brief, requirements, and rubric are on the project page.

4
00:00:22.472 --> 00:00:30.984
This lesson is about the plan: the decisions you make in the first day that determine whether the rest of the week produces evidence or just numbers.

5
00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:39.136
The default task is the one you built in Studio 1: action items from meeting transcripts, with owner, due date, and evidence.

6
00:00:39.416 --> 00:00:44.237
You already have a schema, a verifier, and some labeled examples, which saves days.

7
00:00:44.517 --> 00:00:53.456
You may substitute your own extraction task if you have the data and the right to use it, for example fields from support tickets or contract clauses.

8
00:00:53.736 --> 00:01:01.651
Choose a task with a fixed output schema and fields you can score exactly; free-text summaries make the benchmark much harder to trust.

9
00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:04.213
Notice what the project doesn’t require.

10
00:01:04.493 --> 00:01:07.032
It doesn’t require the fine-tuned model to win.

11
00:01:07.312 --> 00:01:18.939
A benchmark that shows the small model is 5 points worse, or that a cheap API model beats self-hosting at your volume, is a complete and successful project if the evidence is sound.

12
00:01:19.219 --> 00:01:23.848
The rubric rewards the rigor of the comparison, not the direction of the result.
